('24','Historical Evolution of Ownership Pattern and the Malikana System in Bangladesh','historical-evolution-ownership-pattern-malikana-system-bangladesh','property-law','A comprehensive legal history of land ownership in Bangladesh: from pre-Mughal communal tenure through the Permanent Settlement of 1793, Bengal Tenancy Act 1885, East Bengal State Acquisition and Tenancy Act 1950 (zamindari abolition), and the creation of the Malikana system under which every raiyat became a malik holding land directly from the state.','In Bangladesh today, every landholder is called a "malik," meaning owner. Yet this malik is, in strict legal terms, a tenant of the state, obligated to pay annual land development tax under the East Bengal State Acquisition and Tenancy Act, 1950, s 3. Most landholders do not fully appreciate this paradox. The ambiguity is not accidental.

It is the direct product of centuries of successive land tenure systems. Communal cultivation in ancient Bengal gave way to Mughal revenue administration, which yielded to British proprietary experiments, which post-colonial reforms finally dismantled through abolition of intermediaries. The **historical evolution of ownership pattern and the introduction of the Malikana system in Bangladesh** can only be understood by tracing this entire arc.

This article follows that arc: from pre-Mughal communal land through the Permanent Settlement of 1793, the Bengal Tenancy Act, 1885, the East Bengal State Acquisition and Tenancy Act, 1950, and into post-1971 constitutional and legislative reforms under Articles 13, 42, and 143 of the Constitution of Bangladesh. It is written for law students and researchers seeking a consolidated historical account with legislative citations.

## Quick Reference: Major Land Law Milestones in Bangladesh

| Year | Event | Key Effect |
|------|-------|------------|
| 1582 | Todarmal Settlement (Zabt system) | First systematic land measurement; "raiyat" enters official usage |
| 1765 | East India Company acquires Diwani | British inherit Mughal revenue framework intact |
| 1793 | Permanent Settlement (Regulation I of 1793) | Zamindars become absolute proprietors; raiyats reduced to tenants-at-will |
| 1799 | Regulation VII of 1799 | Zamindars granted despotic powers over defaulting tenants |
| 1822 | Regulation VII of 1822 | "Malikana" first used as proprietary allowance (~10%) for displaced zamindars |
| 1873–1875 | Pabna Uprising and Tushkhali Movement | Agrarian unrest triggers colonial reform |
| 1885 | Bengal Tenancy Act | 12-year rule creates occupancy rights; first statutory tenant protection since 1793 |
| 1938 | Floud Commission | Recommends complete abolition of zamindari intermediaries |
| 1950 | East Bengal State Acquisition and Tenancy Act | All intermediaries abolished; raiyats become maliks holding land directly from state |
| 1952–1964 | Zamindari acquisition implemented | Full acquisition completed; major Hindu estates dissolved |
| 1972 | Bangladesh Constitution | Articles 13, 42, 143 enshrine property rights framework |
| 1984 | Land Reform Ordinance | 60-bigha ceiling; sharecropper protections; benami transactions prohibited |

---

## Pre-Mughal Land Tenure in Bengal: Communal Ownership and Early Classifications

The concept of individual land "ownership" as understood in modern jurisprudence was virtually absent in ancient Bengal. Land was a communal resource of the kouma, or clan, classified by use rather than by proprietor. Private title deeds did not exist. The cultivator who cleared and tilled the soil acquired usage rights, not proprietary claims. The [Banglapedia entry on Land Tenure](https://en.banglapedia.org/index.php/Land_Tenure) confirms that this communal structure persisted for centuries before Mughal administrators introduced formal revenue extraction.

Ancient Hindu-period Bengal classified land into three categories. Vastu referred to homestead land, the settled dwelling area of a family or village. Ksetra denoted cultivable plough-land, the productive heart of agrarian life. Khila was waste or fallow land, potentially cultivable but not yet brought under the plough. These classifications organized land by function. They said nothing about who "owned" it.

The king''s role was that of sovereign, not landlord. In theory, the king held ultimate dominion over all land. In practice, his claim was limited to a share of the produce, typically one-sixth of the harvest as revenue. The Arthashastra of Kautilya (approximately 321 BC) recognized that the cultivator who clears and tills land acquires rights to it, subject to this revenue obligation. Religious institutions received nivi-dharma grants, rent-free assignments of land for temples and monasteries, confirming that the king''s interest was fiscal, not possessory.

At the village level, local assemblies managed land allocation and dispute resolution. No intermediary landlord class existed in the later zamindari sense. The village headman coordinated cultivation and revenue collection as a communal functionary, not a proprietor. Land could not be alienated from the village community without collective consent. This decentralized, cultivation-based system of land tenure persisted for centuries across Bengal, shaping a peasant consciousness that would prove resistant to later proprietary impositions.

Buddhist-period Bengal (from roughly the 3rd century BC) added monastic landholding to this picture. Viharas received large tracts as royal grants, introducing institutional land management distinct from both communal and individual tenure. The Pala dynasty (750–1174 AD) formalized such grants through copper-plate inscriptions that survive as some of the earliest documentary evidence of land rights in Bengal.

The transition began when Muslim rulers introduced more formalized revenue extraction. The Sultanate period (1204 onwards) brought bureaucratic elements to land administration that the communal system had never required. Revenue demands became more systematic, collection more centralized. These shifts set the institutional stage for the Mughal era, which would transform Bengal''s agrarian structure far more decisively.

## Mughal Revenue Administration: Zamindars, Jagirs, and the Todarmal Settlement

The word "zamindar" literally means "landholder." Yet under Mughal administration, zamindars held no proprietary title to the land they managed. They were revenue intermediaries, not owners. The state technically owned all land, and the zamindar''s function was collection, not possession.

The Todarmal Settlement of 1582 formalized this arrangement. Emperor Akbar''s finance minister, Raja Todarmal, introduced the Zabt system: systematic land measurement, soil classification, and proportionate revenue assessment that replaced older crop-sharing arrangements. This was the first bureaucratic land revenue system applied to Bengal. The term "raiyat," from the Arabic for "herd at pasture," entered official usage in Todarmal''s settlement to describe the cultivating peasantry.

Two parallel systems distributed revenue rights. Jagir grants were temporary revenue assignments given to officials and military commanders, transferred every three to four years and rarely held by the same person for long. The ijaradari system involved tax farming, where collection rights were auctioned to the highest bidder. Neither system created ownership. Both were mechanisms for extracting and distributing the state''s revenue claim.

The zamindar collected revenue from raiyats on behalf of the Mughal state. In return, he received nankar, a percentage retained for his service. He could not sell or transfer the land itself. He collected three revenue types: mal (agricultural rent), sair (market tolls), and baze-jama (fines and miscellaneous levies). The position was hereditary in practice but legally contingent on the state''s pleasure.

Raiyat classifications created during this period proved remarkably durable. Khudkasta raiyats were permanent residents with hereditary occupancy rights. Paikasta raiyats were non-resident or seasonal cultivators with significantly weaker protections. This distinction persisted into British law and shaped the Bengal Tenancy Act, 1885, three centuries later.

In 1722, Murshid Quli Khan reorganized Bengal into 13 chaklahs and consolidated zamindari holdings, rationalizing the revenue map the British would inherit. When the East India Company acquired the Diwani (revenue collection rights) in 1765, it inherited this Mughal framework intact and maintained it without meaningful reform for seven years before fundamentally altering it.

## The Permanent Settlement of 1793: How Zamindars Became Absolute Proprietors

A single colonial policy decision displaced the customary rights of millions of cultivators and created a landed aristocracy that would exploit the peasantry for over 150 years. Understanding the [Permanent Settlement''s origins and consequences](https://en.banglapedia.org/index.php/Permanent_Settlement,_The) is essential to understanding every land dispute in Bangladesh today.

The East India Company faced a revenue crisis after acquiring the Diwani in 1765. Annual settlements produced unstable revenue. Quinquennial settlements fared no better. Lord Cornwallis sought a permanent solution. The Permanent Settlement Regulation (Regulation I of 1793) fixed the land revenue demand in perpetuity and declared zamindars to be the absolute proprietors of the soil. Scholars have described this as "a grand contract signed between the government of the East India Company in Bengal and individual landholders." In one legislative act, Mughal revenue collectors became British-style landlords with heritable, transferable, and alienable property rights.

The Sun-set Law enforced this arrangement with brutal efficiency. If a zamindar failed to pay the fixed revenue by sunset on the due date, his zamindari was auctioned immediately. By 1799, nearly half of all zamindari properties in Bengal had changed hands through forced sales. The original zamindars, many of whom had held their positions for generations under Mughal rule, were replaced by speculators and moneylenders who understood the new system''s commercial logic.

For raiyats, the consequences were catastrophic. Cultivators became tenants-at-will under zamindars, stripped of any security of tenure. Customary pargana rates, which had limited revenue extraction under the Mughals, were negated. Zamindars imposed illegal levies with impunity: abwabs, tuhuri, dasturi, chandas, bhet, nazrana, and forced labour. Regulation VII of 1799 granted zamindars further despotic powers over defaulting tenants, completing the cultivators'' subjugation.

Sub-infeudation compounded the exploitation. Zamindars sublet to talukdars, who sublet further to other intermediaries, creating multiple layers between the state and the actual cultivator. Each layer extracted its share. The cultivator at the bottom bore the cumulative burden. The Permanent Settlement had created proprietors with every incentive to extract and no incentive to develop. This injustice would take nearly a century to begin correcting.

## Colonial Correctives: Bengal Tenancy Act 1885 and the Floud Commission

It took nearly a century after the Permanent Settlement for the colonial government to acknowledge that zamindars were systematically exploiting raiyats. Even then, reform was cautious and incomplete.

The Bengal Tenancy Act, 1885, was enacted in direct response to agrarian unrest. The Pabna Uprising of 1873 and the Tushkhali Movement of 1872–75 had demonstrated that cultivators would resist when exploitation became intolerable. The colonial administration, alarmed by the scale of rural violence, legislated a partial corrective. The Act created three categories of raiyat: resident raiyats, occupancy raiyats, and non-occupancy raiyats.

The centrepiece was the 12-year rule. A raiyat in continuous possession of land for 12 years acquired an occupancy right that was heritable, transferable as immovable property, and could not be arbitrarily terminated by the zamindar. Some scholarship calls this provision the "Magna Carta of tenants'' rights." It was the first time since 1793 that cultivators received any statutory protection of their tenure.

Subsequent amendments strengthened these protections. The 1928 and 1938/1939 amendments expanded occupancy rights, restricted rent enhancement, and eliminated the requirement that raiyats pay salami (transfer fees) to zamindars. After these amendments, occupancy raiyats could transfer land without obtaining the zamindar''s permission. They had become virtual landowners in all but name.

The Floud Commission of 1938 went further. It examined the zamindari system comprehensively and deemed it untenable. Its central recommendation was radical: abolish the entire intermediary structure and establish a direct relationship between the state and the cultivator. The Commission provided the intellectual blueprint for the East Bengal State Acquisition and Tenancy Act, 1950. World War II and Partition delayed implementation, but the Commission''s analysis proved prophetic.

One colonial-era meaning of "malikana" deserves attention here. Under Regulation VII of 1822, malikana referred to a proprietary allowance of approximately 10 percent of revenue paid to zamindars stripped of administrative roles but retaining a residual proprietary interest. This is distinct from the post-1950 usage. Law students must keep these meanings separate to avoid analytical confusion.

## The East Bengal State Acquisition and Tenancy Act 1950: Abolishing Zamindari and Creating the Malikana System

The ambition of this Act was extraordinary: to eliminate in one stroke the entire hierarchy of intermediaries that the Permanent Settlement had created 157 years earlier. Scholars call it the "Bible of Land Law in Bangladesh." You can read the full text of the [East Bengal State Acquisition and Tenancy Act 1950 at Bangladesh''s official laws portal](http://bdlaws.minlaw.gov.bd/act-325.html), where all amendments are consolidated.

The legislative journey was protracted. The State Acquisition and Tenancy Bill was first introduced on April 10, 1947, months before Partition. After Partition, the new East Bengal legislature reframed and debated the bill extensively. It received assent on May 16, 1951, designated as East Bengal Act XXVIII of 1951. The final statute comprised 152 sections organized across five parts and 19 chapters.

Section 3 is the operative provision. By its operation, all rent-receiving interests were acquired by the state. Every intermediary between the state and the cultivator was abolished. Raiyats became maliks, holders of land directly under the government. The zamindari, talukdari, and all other intermediate tenures ceased to exist.

What does "malik" mean in legal terms? A malik holds land directly from the state, pays land development tax (not rent to a zamindar), and possesses heritable and transferable rights. However, the state retains ultimate ownership. The malik is technically a state tenant, not an allodial owner. The Act vested minerals, subsoil resources, hats, bazaars, forests, fisheries, and ferries in the government.

The Act imposed land ceilings, initially set at 33 acres per family, later adjusted to 100 bighas. Surplus land was to be redistributed to landless cultivators. Major acquisitions occurred between 1952 and 1955, with full acquisition completed by 1964. The implementation gap was severe: only 163,741 acres were actually redistributed, less than one percent of cultivable land. Landlords continued collecting rents illegally for eight years after abolition, and benami (proxy) holdings allowed large landowners to circumvent ceilings. The prohibition of benami transactions was not formally codified until the Land Reform Ordinance, 1984 — a gap that enabled [white-collar property crimes in Bangladesh](/articles/white-collar-crimes-bangladesh-laws-remedies-enforcement) to persist for decades after zamindari abolition.

The social impact was bound to communal politics. Since most East Bengal zamindars were Hindu, the Act''s passage during the communal tensions of 1950 prompted many to migrate to India. The Dhaka Nawab family and the Rajshahi and Puthia Raj families were among the prominent estates dissolved. The Chittagong Hill Tracts were exempted from the Act by gazette notification dated November 30, 1955, preserving customary tribal land tenure in those districts.

Three distinct meanings of "malikana" must be understood. First, the colonial proprietary allowance paid to zamindars. Second, the rent-receiving interest that the 1950 Act abolished. Third, the modern ownership status of maliks under the post-1950 framework. These meanings are historically layered and legally distinct. Conflating them produces analytical error.

## Post-Independence Reforms and the Current Constitutional Framework

Bangladesh''s 1972 Constitution enshrined land reform as a fundamental state objective. Five decades later, the promise remains partly unfulfilled.

Three constitutional provisions define the boundaries. Article 13 recognizes state, cooperative, and private ownership, reflecting the original constitution''s partly socialist orientation. Article 42 guarantees the right to acquire, hold, transfer, and dispose of property, subject to law. This is the constitutional anchor of the malik''s rights, elevating the 1950 Act''s statutory tenure into a constitutionally protected interest. Article 143 vests all minerals, subterranean waters, and "ownerless" property in the state. For a broader analysis of how constitutional supremacy operates in Bangladesh''s legal order, see our companion article on [why the constitution is the supreme law of the land](/articles/constitution-supreme-law-land).

The Land Reform Ordinance, 1984, was the most significant post-independence reform. It reduced the land ceiling to 60 bighas per family for agricultural land, introduced formal protections for sharecroppers (bargadars) for the first time (requiring written five-year contracts and mandating a one-third crop division between landowner, sharecropper, and inputs), and prohibited benami transactions. Families holding under 25 bighas had already been exempted from revenue payments since April 1972.

The malik''s right to inherit and transmit land is now one of the most practically significant aspects of this tenure. When a malik dies, their land passes according to the applicable personal law — Muslim, Hindu, or Christian — and understanding which succession system applies is essential for modern title verification. Our detailed guide to [inheritance law in Bangladesh across Hindu, Muslim, and Christian succession systems](/articles/inheritance-law-bangladesh-hindu-muslim-christian) addresses this question directly for practitioners and families navigating title transmission today.

The malik''s right to transfer land inter vivos (between living parties) is governed primarily by the Transfer of Property Act 1882 as applied in Bangladesh. The foundational definitions in [Sections 3 to 41 of the Transfer of Property Act 1882](/articles/transfer-of-property-act-1882-sections-3-41-bangladesh) — covering immovable property, transfer, notice, and attestation requirements — apply to every malik-to-malik conveyance. The 2026 amendments creating [Sections 122A and 122B on lifetime usufruct retention and Heba](/articles/sections-122a-122b-tpa-1882-bangladesh-2026-lifetime-usufruct-heba) represent the most recent legislative development in this area.

Khas land distribution remains a persistent policy aspiration. Total estimated khas land stands at approximately 3.3 million acres: 0.8 million acres of agricultural land, 1.7 million acres of non-agricultural land, and 0.8 million acres of water bodies. Distribution to landless families has been limited by elite capture, local power structures, and administrative inefficiency. The gap between policy and implementation here mirrors the 1950 Act''s redistribution record.

The current ceiling stands at 100 standard bighas in aggregate, with 60 bighas for agricultural holdings specifically under the 1984 Ordinance.

The unresolved tension at the heart of Bangladeshi land law is structural. Maliks hold heritable, transferable rights. They can mortgage, lease, and dispose of their land. Yet the state can acquire land for any public purpose, subject to compensation. The malik''s position is stronger than any raiyat''s ever was, but weaker than freehold ownership in common law systems. The East Bengal State Acquisition and Tenancy Act, 1950, remains the foundational statute, amended repeatedly but never replaced.

## Why This History Matters for Modern Land Disputes

Over 80 percent of civil litigation in Bangladesh''s district courts involves land disputes. Understanding why requires tracing the unresolved legacies of each historical system described above.

**Title chain complexity.** Because zamindars were abolished mid-20th century and records were converted at acquisition, many title chains carry pre-1950 zamindari documents, colonial patta-kabuliyat records, and post-1950 mutation entries — sometimes inconsistently. Courts must reconstruct ownership history across three distinct legal regimes.

**Benami and proxy holdings.** The practice of registering land in names of fictitious or nominal owners — enabled after zamindari abolition and not criminalized until 1984 — has left disputed title trails in millions of parcels. Many land disputes before Bangladesh''s courts today are traced to ceiling circumvention strategies used in the 1950s and 1960s.

**Khas land encroachment.** Land that vested in the government on acquisition but was never distributed or formally demarcated is routinely encroached upon. Competing claims between state khas land and private malik records generate protracted litigation.

**Sharecropper dispossession.** Despite the 1984 Ordinance''s protections, bargadars (sharecroppers) are routinely evicted without due process. Their written contract requirement is frequently ignored, and the courts have not consistently enforced the statutory five-year security.

Practitioners advising clients on property matters in Bangladesh must therefore verify not just the current RS and BS khatian entries, but trace the acquisition record, verify whether the land was exempted from acquisition (Chittagong Hill Tracts), and check for any vested property (former enemy/vested property) status. The [land registration process in Bangladesh — step by step](/articles/land-registration-process-bangladesh-step-by-step) guide covers the current verification workflow from mutation through sub-registry.

## Comparison Table: Zamindari, Raiyati, and Malikana Systems

| Feature | Zamindari (1793–1950) | Raiyati (1885–1950) | Malikana (1950–present) |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Legal basis** | Permanent Settlement, Regulation I of 1793 | Bengal Tenancy Act, 1885 | East Bengal State Acquisition and Tenancy Act, 1950, s 3 |
| **Relationship to state** | Absolute proprietor (freehold-like) | Tenant of zamindar | Direct tenant of state |
| **Heritable?** | Yes | Yes (occupancy raiyat) | Yes |
| **Transferable?** | Yes (alienable) | Yes (after 1938 amendments) | Yes (subject to ceiling) |
| **Revenue obligation** | Fixed revenue to state (Permanent Settlement) | Rent to zamindar | Land development tax to government |
| **Intermediary above?** | None (above the cultivator) | Zamindar and sub-tenants | None |
| **Land ceiling?** | No | No | Yes (60 bighas agricultural; 100 bighas total) |
| **Subsoil rights?** | Technically yes, in practice contested | No | No (vested in state) |
| **Can be acquired by state?** | Yes, on payment of compensation | N/A | Yes, on payment of compensation (Art. 42) |

