## FAQ

### What is the Malikana system in Bangladesh?

The Malikana system is the post-1950 land tenure framework in which former raiyats (cultivators) became maliks (owners) holding land directly from the state. The East Bengal State Acquisition and Tenancy Act, 1950, s 3, abolished all intermediary rent-receiving interests. Maliks pay land development tax to the government rather than rent to zamindars, and hold heritable and transferable rights.

### What is the difference between a zamindar and a malik?

Zamindars were intermediary proprietors created by the Permanent Settlement of 1793, standing between the state and the cultivator. Maliks are direct state tenants created by the 1950 Act after zamindari abolition. The key distinction is structural: no intermediary exists between a malik and the state. The malik pays land development tax directly and owes nothing to any intermediary.

### Did the Permanent Settlement of 1793 create private land ownership in Bengal?

Yes, but only for zamindars. Regulation I of 1793 converted Mughal-era revenue collectors into absolute proprietors with heritable, transferable, and alienable rights. Raiyats received no ownership rights and were reduced to tenants-at-will. Cultivators gained no statutory tenure protections until the Bengal Tenancy Act, 1885, and its 12-year occupancy rule.

### Does a malik in Bangladesh have absolute ownership of land?

No. A malik holds the strongest form of tenure available under Bangladeshi law but is technically a state tenant. The state retains ultimate ownership under Article 143 of the Constitution. The government can acquire a malik''s land for public purposes, subject to compensation under Article 42. Maliks cannot claim rights to subsoil resources or minerals. They must also pay annual land development tax and comply with land ceiling restrictions under the Land Reform Ordinance, 1984.

### What legislation currently governs land ownership in Bangladesh?

The primary statute is the East Bengal State Acquisition and Tenancy Act, 1950 (as amended), which establishes the malik as the basic unit of land tenure. This is supplemented by the Land Reform Ordinance, 1984 (addressing land ceilings, sharecropper protections, benami transactions, and khas land distribution) and Articles 13, 42, and 143 of the Constitution of Bangladesh, which provide the overarching framework recognizing private property rights while reserving ultimate state dominion.

### What were the three meanings of "malikana" under Bengali and Bangladesh law?

The word "malikana" carries three historically distinct legal meanings. First, under Regulation VII of 1822, it denoted a proprietary allowance of roughly 10 percent paid to zamindars who lost administrative roles but retained residual proprietary interest. Second, under the East Bengal State Acquisition and Tenancy Act, 1950, the word described the rent-receiving interests that were abolished by the state acquisition. Third, in contemporary usage, "malikana" describes the ownership status — the bundle of heritable, transferable rights — held by modern maliks under the post-1950 framework. Conflating these three usages in legal argument produces analytical error. The [Contract Act 1872 as applied in Bangladesh](/articles/contract-act-1872-bangladesh-explained-practitioners-guide) provides useful parallels for understanding how colonial-era statutory concepts have been reinterpreted by Bangladeshi courts after independence.

### What is the land ceiling in Bangladesh today?

Under the Land Reform Ordinance, 1984, no family may hold more than 60 bighas of agricultural land. The aggregate ceiling across all land types is 100 standard bighas. Land held in excess of these ceilings is liable to vest in the government as khas land for redistribution to landless families. These ceilings operate alongside the constitutional property protections under Article 42.',
